---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getContent, worldviewUrl } from '../lib/content';
import { url } from '../lib/paths';

const { worldviews, questions } = await getContent();
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Method"
  description="How worldviews are compared: the steelman rule, the comparison structure, and the standpoint of the site."
>
  <div class="container page">
    <h1>Method</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        The site asks which worldview gives the most coherent account of reality as
        actually encountered: existence, knowledge, logic, personality, morality, beauty,
        evil, guilt, suffering, death, history, and human destiny.
      </p>

      <h2>Standpoint</h2>
      <p>
        The site is written from a confessional Reformed Christian standpoint. It does
        not claim a neutral position above all worldviews. At the same time, every
        competing worldview must be presented in a form that a serious adherent or
        scholar could recognize as fair.
      </p>

      <h2>The six comparison lanes</h2>
      <ol>
        {worldviews.map((w) => <li><a href={worldviewUrl(w)}>{w.name}</a></li>)}
      </ol>
      <p>
        These are comparison families, not claims of total internal uniformity. Internal
        diversity is surfaced whenever it materially changes an answer.
      </p>

      <h2>The steelman rule</h2>
      <p>Before any Christian critique is written, the entry must answer:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>What does this worldview actually claim?</li>
        <li>Which major school or thinker is being represented?</li>
        <li>What explanatory work does the view genuinely perform well?</li>
        <li>Are important internal alternatives being omitted?</li>
        <li>Can the steelman be supported from a primary source or serious internal authority?</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>How entries are structured</h2>
      <p>
        Entries for non-Christian worldviews present the <strong>View</strong>,
        <strong>What it explains well</strong>, the <strong>Christian response</strong>,
        and a <strong>Pressure point</strong>. Entries for Christianity present the
        <strong>Christian view</strong>, <strong>What it explains well</strong>, the
        <strong>Strongest objection</strong>, and the <strong>Christian reply</strong>.
        Christianity is tested too.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Primary, canonical, revelatory, confessional, or foundational texts form the base
        representation of each worldview. Authoritative interpretive standards and major
        historical interpreters come next, followed by later thinkers and academic scholarship.
        Scripture is the primary and final authority for the Reformed lane; confessions
        and theologians are subordinate witnesses. A hostile secondary source is never the sole authority for
        what another tradition teaches. See the <a href={url('sources/')}>source registry</a>
        for the full hierarchy.
      </p>
      <p>
        Citations appear as small numerals in the text. Each opens a preview of its reference and
        links to its numbered entry in the question's Sources list, which links back. The same work
        can be cited many times with different locators.
      </p>

      <h2>Reading a question</h2>
      <p>Each of the {questions.length} questions can be read in three ways.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Explore</strong> shows all six answers at a glance; any one opens to its full
          account, the analysis and its sources.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Compare</strong> sets any two answers side by side, section by section, without
          privileging either.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Christian Case</strong> reads one alternative from the Reformed standpoint, in
          stages: its own account and strengths, the Christian account, the Christian response, the
          questions put to it, and the strongest objection to the Christian view with its reply.
          That objection is stated in the Christian answer; it is not the alternative's reply to
          the Christian response, and it may come from another tradition.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Authority, history, and remedy</h2>
      <p>
        Each worldview is represented according to its recognized sources of authority,
        including its standards for adjudicating fundamental disagreements. Diversity alone
        is not a refutation. Naturalism's lack of a canon is a structural feature; its own
        methods of adjudication must be considered.
      </p>
      <p>
        Historical claims are evaluated historically. Internal authority explains belief
        but does not establish historical fact. Chronology, source proximity, textual
        attestation, context, and explanatory power matter; contradictory claims are not
        evidentially equal merely because each belongs to a tradition. The Jesus question
        examines both each worldview's claims and how they stand against historical evidence.
      </p>
      <p>
        A proposed remedy is tested first against its worldview's own diagnosis of the human
        problem, then comparatively against reality. The salvation anchor follows diagnosis,
        remedy, means, grace or effort, and final state. The question map remains fixed at 28.
      </p>

      <h2>Tone</h2>
      <p>
        Precise and non-mocking, with no triumphalist scoring and no numeric worldview
        scores. The site distinguishes what another worldview says, what historians and
        textual scholars establish, and what the Christian concludes from those facts.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
